\title{Proofs as Directed Acyclic Graphs: Conservation, Hubs, and Foundations in Dependency Networks}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-10}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop the elementary structural theory of \emph{dependency networks} --- directed graphs in which vertices are mathematical statements and a directed edge $u \to v$ records that statement $u$ is used in the derivation of statement $v$. We isolate three foundational invariants. First, a \emph{conservation law}: in any finite network the aggregate in-degree, the aggregate out-degree, and the total number of edges all coincide, the directed analogue of the handshaking lemma. Second, a \emph{hub-existence principle}: as an immediate pigeonhole consequence of conservation, every nonempty network contains a statement whose in-degree is at least the network-wide average, equivalently a single node whose in-degree bounds the entire edge budget from below. Third, an \emph{order-and-foundation theorem}: acyclicity --- the logical requirement that no statement transitively depends on itself --- makes the transitive ``eventually depends on'' relation a strict partial order, and on a finite network this forces the existence of \emph{sources} (dependency-free foundations) and \emph{sinks} (frontier statements). We give complete proof sketches, dual formulations for in- and out-degrees, and discuss how these invariants underpin the empirical observation that comprehensive mathematical corpora are scale-free, with in-degree distribution $P(k)\sim k^{-\gamma}$, $\gamma \approx 2.5$. We close with algorithmic recipes, numerical demonstrations, and a program of conjectures on rank stratification, super-average hub concentration, and network fragility under hub deletion.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Mathematical knowledge is cumulative in a strong and literal sense: essentially every theorem is proved by invoking earlier theorems, which invoke earlier ones still, terminating at axioms and definitions. This \emph{dependency} relation defines a directed graph on the set of all statements. The graph is not an informal metaphor; it is a combinatorial object with quantifiable structure, and the aim of this paper is to establish its most basic invariants rigorously and to explain how those invariants shape the large-scale statistics observed in comprehensive mathematical libraries.

Two forces govern the object. One is \emph{arithmetic}: edges must be conserved, and a fixed edge budget shared among a fixed number of nodes forces concentration. The other is \emph{logical}: a proof cannot be circular, so the graph is acyclic, which endows it with an order and, on finite data, with a bottom layer of foundations. From these two forces we derive a small collection of theorems that are simple to state, exact, and the correct starting point for a quantitative science of mathematical structure.

Our contributions are: a precise model of dependency networks (Section~\ref{sec:model}); the conservation law in target and source forms (Section~\ref{sec:conservation}); the hub-existence theorem and its dual (Section~\ref{sec:hubs}); the acyclicity theory, with sources and sinks (Section~\ref{sec:acyclic}); and a discussion connecting these invariants to scale-free statistics, algorithms, and conjectures (Sections~\ref{sec:scalefree}--\ref{sec:discussion}).

\section{Related structural background}

The undirected handshaking lemma --- that the sum of vertex degrees is twice the number of edges --- is a staple of graph theory. Our conservation law is its directed refinement, splitting each incidence into a source-count and a target-count. The existence of sources and sinks in finite directed acyclic graphs is classical and underlies topological sorting; we reconstruct it from the well-foundedness of an irreflexive transitive relation on a finite set. The empirical claim that citation- and dependency-style graphs are scale-free follows a long line of work on complex networks, in which power-law degree distributions and hub-dominated topologies recur across technological, biological, and social domains. Our contribution is to ground the \emph{necessary preconditions} for such topologies --- conservation and forced concentration --- in exact, general theorems.

\section{The model}\label{sec:model}

Fix a finite type $V$ of \textbf{statements}. A \textbf{dependency network} on $V$ is a binary relation $R$ on $V$ with $R\,u\,v$ interpreted as ``statement $u$ is used directly in the derivation of statement $v$.'' We assume $R$ is decidable so that degrees and edge sets are genuine finite cardinalities.

\begin{definition}[Degrees]
For $v \in V$,
\[
\indeg(v) := \#\{\, u \in V : R\,u\,v \,\}, \qquad \outdeg(v) := \#\{\, u \in V : R\,v\,u \,\}.
\]
We call $\indeg(v)$ the \emph{in-degree} (the number of statements used directly to prove $v$) and $\outdeg(v)$ the \emph{out-degree} (the number of statements that directly use $v$).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Edges]
The \emph{edge set} is $E(R) := \{\, (u,v) \in V \times V : R\,u\,v \,\}$, with \emph{edge count} $m := \#\,E(R)$. The \emph{order} of the network is $n := \#V$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Transitive dependence]
Write $u \Rightarrow v$ if there is a finite chain $u = x_0, x_1, \dots, x_k = v$ with $k \ge 1$ and $R\,x_i\,x_{i+1}$ for each $i$. This is the transitive closure of $R$: ``$u$ is used, directly or indirectly, in the derivation of $v$.''
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Acyclicity]
The network $R$ is \emph{acyclic} if no statement transitively depends on itself: for all $v$, it is not the case that $v \Rightarrow v$.
\end{definition}

Acyclicity is the exact combinatorial content of the ban on circular reasoning: a purported proof in which $v \Rightarrow v$ would derive $v$ using $v$, which is no proof at all.

\section{The conservation law}\label{sec:conservation}

\begin{theorem}[Conservation, target form]\label{thm:cons-target}
For any finite dependency network, $\displaystyle \sum_{v \in V} \indeg(v) = m$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Partition the edge set $E(R)$ according to the target coordinate. The fiber over $v$ is $\{(u,v) : R\,u\,v\}$, whose size is exactly $\indeg(v)$ since $u \mapsto (u,v)$ is a bijection from the in-neighbours of $v$ to that fiber. Summing fiber sizes recovers $\#E(R) = m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Conservation, source form]\label{thm:cons-source}
For any finite dependency network, $\displaystyle \sum_{v \in V} \outdeg(v) = m$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Identical, partitioning $E(R)$ by the source coordinate; the fiber over $v$ has size $\outdeg(v)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Incidence conservation]
$\displaystyle \sum_{v} \indeg(v) = \sum_{v} \outdeg(v)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Both sides equal $m$ by Theorems~\ref{thm:cons-target} and~\ref{thm:cons-source}.
\end{proof}

This is the directed handshaking identity: every dependency contributes exactly one incoming and one outgoing incidence, so the two global tallies agree.

\section{Hubs are inevitable}\label{sec:hubs}

\begin{theorem}[Hub existence]\label{thm:hub}
In any nonempty dependency network ($n \ge 1$) there exists a statement $v^{\ast}$ with
\[
m \le n \cdot \indeg(v^{\ast}).
\]
Equivalently, $\indeg(v^{\ast}) \ge m/n$: some statement has in-degree at least the network-wide average.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Choose $v^{\ast}$ to maximize $\indeg$ over the nonempty vertex set. For every $v$, $\indeg(v) \le \indeg(v^{\ast})$, so
\[
m = \sum_v \indeg(v) \le \sum_v \indeg(v^{\ast}) = n \cdot \indeg(v^{\ast}),
\]
using Theorem~\ref{thm:cons-target} for the first equality.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Dual hub existence]
In any nonempty dependency network there exists $w^{\ast}$ with $m \le n \cdot \outdeg(w^{\ast})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:hub} to the reversed relation $R^{\op}\,u\,v := R\,v\,u$, under which in-degrees become out-degrees and the edge count is unchanged (edge reversal $(u,v)\mapsto(v,u)$ is a bijection of edge sets).
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Sharpness]
The bound is tight: if every in-degree equals a common value $d$, then $m = nd$ and $\indeg(v^{\ast}) = d$, so $m = n\cdot\indeg(v^{\ast})$. The inequality is thus the exact statement that maximum in-degree dominates the average, with equality precisely for in-degree-regular networks.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[The scale-free regime]
Theorem~\ref{thm:hub} has bite exactly when $m$ is large relative to $n$. In a mature corpus, results accumulate faster than the foundational lemmas they cite, so $m$ grows super-linearly in $n$; then $\indeg(v^{\ast}) \ge m/n$ grows without bound, and equal sharing of the edge budget is impossible. Concentration into hubs is forced, not accidental.
\end{remark}

\section{Acyclicity, order, and foundations}\label{sec:acyclic}

Throughout this section $R$ is acyclic.

\begin{lemma}[Direct irreflexivity]
If $R$ is acyclic then $R\,v\,v$ fails for every $v$: no statement is an immediate premise of itself.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
A single edge $R\,v\,v$ is a length-one chain witnessing $v \Rightarrow v$, contradicting acyclicity.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Proof order]\label{thm:order}
If $R$ is acyclic, the transitive dependence relation $\Rightarrow$ is a strict partial order on $V$: irreflexive (by acyclicity) and transitive (concatenation of dependence chains).
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Irreflexivity is the definition of acyclicity. Transitivity holds because a chain from $u$ to $v$ followed by a chain from $v$ to $w$ is a chain from $u$ to $w$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Existence of foundations / sources]\label{thm:source}
Let $V$ be finite and nonempty and $R$ acyclic. Then there exists $v \in V$ with no incoming dependency: for all $u$, $R\,u\,v$ fails.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:order}, $\Rightarrow$ is transitive and irreflexive. A transitive, irreflexive relation on a finite set is well-founded --- no infinite descending chains. Hence $\Rightarrow$ has a minimal element $v$ over the nonempty set: no $u$ satisfies $u \Rightarrow v$, and in particular no $u$ satisfies $R\,u\,v$. So $v$ is a source.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Existence of frontiers / sinks]
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:source} there exists $v \in V$ with no outgoing dependency: for all $u$, $R\,v\,u$ fails.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:source} to $R^{\op}$; its transitive closure is the reverse of $\Rightarrow$, still irreflexive, so $R^{\op}$ is acyclic, and a source of $R^{\op}$ is a sink of $R$.
\end{proof}

\noindent\textbf{Interpretation.} Theorem~\ref{thm:source} is the exact sense in which mathematics rests on axioms: following dependency arrows backward from any theorem, finiteness bars infinite regress and acyclicity bars loops, so the walk halts at a statement with nothing behind it --- an axiom or definition. Symmetrically the sinks form the \emph{frontier} of newest results. Both theorems are non-vacuous: a network with a two-cycle $a \to b \to a$ has neither a source nor a sink, so acyclicity is load-bearing.

\section{A worked example}\label{sec:example}

To make the invariants concrete, consider a network of six statements $\{0,1,2,3,4,5\}$ with dependency edges
\[
0\to2,\quad 1\to2,\quad 0\to3,\quad 1\to3,\quad 2\to4,\quad 3\to4,\quad 4\to5.
\]
Here $0$ and $1$ are unproved primitives, $2$ and $3$ are lemmas each drawing on both primitives, $4$ is a theorem combining the two lemmas, and $5$ is a corollary. The edge count is $m = 7$.

The in-degrees $0,0,2,2,2,1$ sum to $7 = m$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:cons-target}); the out-degrees $2,2,1,1,1,0$ also sum to $7 = m$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:cons-source} and its corollary). Hub existence (Theorem~\ref{thm:hub}) is witnessed by $v^{\ast} = 2$ with $\indeg(2)=2$, certifying $m = 7 \le n\cdot\indeg(v^{\ast}) = 6\cdot 2 = 12$; the dual hub is the most-used primitive $0$ with $\outdeg(0)=2$. Topological layering yields the strata $\{0,1\} \prec \{2,3\} \prec \{4\} \prec \{5\}$, so the sources (Theorem~\ref{thm:source}) are the foundations $\{0,1\}$ and the unique sink is the frontier $\{5\}$. Adding a back-edge $5\to0$ creates the cycle $0\to2\to4\to5\to0$; layering then stalls and the network has neither a source nor a sink, exactly as acyclicity predicts.

\section{From invariants to scale-free statistics}\label{sec:scalefree}

The three invariants combine: conservation fixes the global edge budget; hub existence shows a heavy budget cannot be shared equally; acyclicity stratifies the network from foundations to frontier, so a hub's influence propagates upward into everything above it. Empirically, dependency networks from large corpora exhibit an in-degree distribution
\[
P(k) \sim k^{-\gamma}, \qquad \gamma \approx 2.5,
\]
the hallmark of a \emph{scale-free network}: most statements depend on a few results, while rare hubs are depended upon by exponentially many. Canonical hub candidates are the reflexively cited results of mathematics: Zorn's Lemma, the Intermediate Value Theorem, the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, the Sylow Theorems, the Baire Category Theorem, the Hahn--Banach Theorem, Urysohn's Lemma, the Pigeonhole Principle, induction, and the law of excluded middle. The hub theorems guarantee such nodes must exist; the empirical power law measures how heavy their tail is.

Scale-free graphs are robust to random node loss but fragile under targeted hub removal. Here deleting a hub removes not one vertex but the support of everything built above it, the structural mechanism behind the conjecture that removing a single top hub fractures the network into large components.

\section{Algorithms}\label{sec:algorithms}

\noindent\textbf{Degree and conservation audit.} Compute $\indeg$, $\outdeg$, and $m$ in one pass, and verify $\sum_v \indeg(v) = m = \sum_v \outdeg(v)$. Cost $O(n+m)$ on sparse data.

\noindent\textbf{Hub extraction.} Scan the in-degree array for its maximum $v^{\ast}$ and certify $m \le n\cdot\indeg(v^{\ast})$. Cost $O(n)$.

\noindent\textbf{Topological layering.} Repeatedly peel in-degree-$0$ vertices (Kahn's algorithm); the peel order is a topological sort, sources are the first layer, sinks are out-degree-$0$ vertices, and a stall certifies a cycle. Cost $O(n+m)$.

\noindent\textbf{Power-law fit.} From the in-degree multiset, estimate the tail exponent by maximum likelihood,
\[
\hat\gamma = 1 + N_{\ge k_{\min}} \Big/ \sum_{k \ge k_{\min}} \ln\!\frac{k}{k_{\min} - \tfrac12}.
\]

\section{Discussion and future directions}\label{sec:discussion}

The framework recasts qualitative intuitions as measurable quantities: ``foundational'' becomes high out-degree and low rank; ``deep'' becomes distance from the sources; ``load-bearing'' becomes fragility cost under deletion. The same laws apply to any justificatory network --- legal precedent, software dependencies, scientific citation.

\begin{conjecture}[Rank stratification]
Every finite acyclic network admits a rank function $\rho : V \to \mathbb{N}$ with $\rho(u) < \rho(v)$ whenever $u \to v$, and the number of distinct ranks equals the length of the longest derivation chain.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Super-average hub concentration]
If $m \ge c\,n^{1+\delta}$ for constants $c,\delta > 0$, then the maximum in-degree is at least $c\,n^{\delta}$, and iterating the hub bound on residual networks yields a descending ladder of hubs.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Hub-deletion fragility]
There is a constant $\alpha > 0$ such that deleting any statement of in-degree exceeding $\alpha\,m/n$ leaves a residual network whose largest weakly-connected component omits a constant fraction of all statements.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Limitations and modelling assumptions}\label{sec:limits}

Three modelling choices deserve comment. First, we treat the dependency relation as given and decidable; the granularity of what counts as a single statement affects degree counts, and cross-corpus comparisons require a consistent choice, though our theorems are invariant under any fixed one. Second, acyclicity is a property of proofs, not of statements: two theorems may be logically equivalent even though no single proof is circular, and a faithful dependency network records the derivation actually used. Third, the empirical exponent $\gamma \approx 2.5$ is a measured quantity depending on the corpus and the tail cutoff $k_{\min}$; our theorems guarantee the existence of hubs and foundations unconditionally, but the heaviness of the tail is an empirical input, not a theorem. These caveats delimit the scope of the results without weakening them.

\section{Conclusion}

Viewing proofs as a directed acyclic graph exposes three exact laws: dependencies are conserved, hubs are forced, and finiteness with acyclicity guarantees foundations and a frontier. These are the minimal structural facts any dependency network must obey, and they supply the scaffolding on which the empirical scale-free geometry of mathematics rests. Mathematics is not a heap of facts but a network with a blueprint, and the blueprint is a graph whose most connected nodes hold the entire structure together.

